Weekly backlog burndown: in the ~10 hours before the account's weekly Claude usage reset, spend the remaining budget implementing backlog items across The Borg. The job fires twice because the rolling 5-hour session limit caps what one run can burn: the first run typically dies at the session limit, and the second starts with a fresh session window and resumes the same plan. The fire times themselves live in `.bin/install-scheduled-tasks.sh` and are deliberately not repeated here — a schedule copied into a prompt drifts silently the moment the job is rescheduled.

This job runs headless via `claude -p` from `${BORG_ROOT}/c4po`. `.bin/run-scheduled-task.sh` gives it `--add-dir ${BORG_ROOT}` (it edits trackers across the whole workspace, including the git-ignored `repos/*`) and `--permission-mode bypassPermissions` (it must write and commit unattended; a mid-BURN permission prompt cannot be answered and would end the run having implemented nothing). It is EXPECTED to die mid-run when the weekly budget runs out — every phase therefore leaves durable state (commits, checked boxes) before moving on, and the plan email goes out before implementation starts.

Until 2026-08-15 this job ran on `codex exec` inside an OS-enforced workspace-write Seatbelt sandbox, and that boundary is the one thing the move to Claude did not carry over. Two consequences are load-bearing for the rules below. First, nothing outside this prompt confines a child session to its item's repository — scope is enforced by the self-contained child prompt, the recorded-SHA range, and commit reconciliation, so those steps are not ceremony. Second, `bypassPermissions` skips the `Edit` deny rules in `c4po/.claude/settings.local.json`, including the ones protecting `cerebruh/`; the prohibition on writing wiki content is therefore instruction-only here and must be restated in every child prompt.

STANDING RULE FOR THE WHOLE RUN — never change branches, in any repository, in any phase. Every commit this job makes (reordered backlogs in PRIORITIZE, tracker updates in BURN) goes onto the branch already checked out in that repository. Do not create, switch, rename, or delete a branch and do not commit to a detached HEAD, regardless of any default guidance about branching before committing on a default branch. That guidance is Claude Code's normal behavior and it is wrong here: this job's whole contract is that its work is on `main` when the user wakes up, and a side branch is indistinguishable from work that never happened. The same rule is restated for child sessions in BURN because a child does not inherit this text.

The phases below are named, not numbered, so the paired `/backlog-burndown` command can reference them without breaking when this file is edited.

GATE — decide the run mode: FRESH, RESUME, or stop.
Determine the current ISO week with `date +%G-W%V` and read `${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/state/c4po-backlog-burndown.json` (if present).
- State file missing, or its `week` field is not the current ISO week → **FRESH**: run every phase below.
- `week` equals the current ISO week and the plan file (`${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/state/c4po-backlog-burndown-plan.md`) still has unchecked actionable items → **RESUME**: skip PRIORITIZE, CONSOLIDATE, NOTIFY PLAN, and RECORD STATE; go straight to BURN at the first unchecked item. A prior run this week already planned, emailed, and recorded state — this run only continues the burn.
- `week` equals the current ISO week and the plan has no unchecked actionable items (or the plan file is missing) → stop and output nothing; the week's plan is spent.

WINDOW — abort if fired outside the pre-reset window.
Two facts drive this phase, and neither is a schedule:
- **Weekly usage reset: Wednesday 11:00 AM local.** An account fact, read from `/usage` in an interactive Claude Code session. This is the one timing value that belongs in this file.
- **Rolling session limit: 5 hours.** A vendor fact, re-verified monthly by the assumptions audit (Assumption H).

Derive the valid window from those two — do not pin fire times here, and do not read them from the scheduler at run time either; the bounds below hold regardless of when launchd actually fires:
- earliest valid start = reset − (2 x session window) − 20min slack
- latest valid start  = reset − 30min

With today's values that is **Wednesday 00:40 through Wednesday 10:30** (derived, not authoritative — recompute from the two facts above rather than trusting this line). If the current local time is outside it, stop and output nothing: launchd fires missed events on wake, so a sleeping Mac can delay a run past the reset, and running after the reset would burn the fresh week's budget, which defeats the purpose.

Two session windows is the sizing rationale — the job is built to fill roughly two consecutive session windows before the reset, which is also why it fires twice. If the account's reset time changes, update the reset fact above and re-derive the schedule in `.bin/install-scheduled-tasks.sh` from it; the assumptions audit verifies the two stay consistent.

PRIORITIZE — reorder every backlog in place.
Enumerate the backlogs by scanning the filesystem, never from memory or from a previous run's plan: `${BORG_ROOT}/BACKLOG.md` (workspace) and every `${BORG_ROOT}/repos/*/BACKLOG.md` (`repos/` is git-ignored, so its children never appear in workspace git status). At the time of writing that resolves to exactly two files — the workspace backlog and `repos/waiq/BACKLOG.md` — but the scan is authoritative and repos come and go; if it finds a different set, work the set it found and say so. Record the enumerated paths and each file's open-item count before reordering anything; CONSOLIDATE must account for every one of them. Reorder each file's open items by priority, preserving that file's own format and conventions (the workspace file is a flat checkbox list — reorder it highest-priority-first; a repo file with priority tiers keeps its tiers, moving items between tiers only when clearly warranted). Priority rubric: user pain in active use > bugs > security/correctness > features > nice-to-haves; weigh what unblocks other items. Do not rewrite item content. Commit each reordered file in its own repo (workspace file in the theborg repo, repo files in their repos).

CONSOLIDATE — build the run plan.
Write `${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/state/c4po-backlog-burndown-plan.md` (overwriting any previous run's plan): a single prioritized union of all open items across every backlog PRIORITIZE enumerated, one checkbox line per item with its source file, owning agent/repo, and dispatch directory (per BURN). Items a headless run cannot do get a `needs-user:` marker with a one-line reason instead of a checkbox — anything requiring a physical device, user-only verification, credentials or purchases, app-store/publishing actions, or a destructive/irreversible operation. Order the actionable rest by the same priority rubric.

The union is ranked strictly across sources, not grouped by source. Rank every item against every other item regardless of which backlog it came from: a workspace P-equivalent bug outranks a repo nice-to-have and vice versa. Per-file order from PRIORITIZE is an input, not a constraint — do not concatenate the files, and do not let one backlog's items monopolize the top merely because that file is longer or uses explicit tiers while another does not. A run of consecutive same-source items is fine when the rubric earns it; a plan whose order is explainable purely by source file is a merge failure, so re-rank before writing.

Open the plan with a coverage line for each enumerated backlog — `<path>: <open items found> found, <actionable> actionable, <needs-user> needs-user` — and the totals. Every enumerated backlog gets a row even when it contributed zero items, which is what makes a missed or empty backlog visible instead of silent. If a row's found count does not equal its actionable + needs-user counts, the merge dropped items: fix it before writing.

NOTIFY PLAN — email the plan before burning.
Pipe the plan into `${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/notify-email.sh c4po "Borg backlog burndown — {YYYY-MM-DD} — plan: {N} item(s), {K} needs-user"` (reads the message from stdin). Body header: same as the subject. This email is sent BEFORE implementation because the run is expected to die when the weekly budget runs out; it is the user's record of what the run set out to do.

RECORD STATE — mark the week done before burning.
Write `${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/state/c4po-backlog-burndown.json` with `{"week":"YYYY-Www","date":"YYYY-MM-DD","ts":"<ISO 8601 timestamp>","planned":<N>,"needs_user":<K>}`. `mkdir -p` the state directory first if needed. State is recorded now, not at the end, because the end may never arrive.

BURN — implement items until the weekly budget runs out.
Loop over the plan top-down, dispatching each item to a FRESH headless child session — one `claude -p` per item. This keeps any item from paying for the context accumulated by the ones before it (context rot and token cost both grow with session length), and it loads the correct AGENTS.md chain for the item's owner. This session is the orchestrator only: it spawns children, verifies evidence, and updates trackers — it does not implement items itself.
- Dispatch directory: workspace item owned by an agent → `${BORG_ROOT}/<agent>`; owned by the workspace itself → `${BORG_ROOT}`; repo item → that repo's root under `${BORG_ROOT}/repos/`.
- Spawn: immediately before starting each child, record BOTH `git -C "<dispatch repo>" rev-parse HEAD` and `git -C "<dispatch repo>" rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD` for that item. The branch is recorded because Claude Code's default git guidance is to create a branch before committing when the session is on the default branch — see the branch rule below. Then, from the dispatch directory, run:
  `claude -p "<child prompt>" --model opus --effort high --permission-mode bypassPermissions --strict-mcp-config`
  - `bypassPermissions` is required: a child cannot answer a prompt, so any permission gate ends it having done nothing. `--strict-mcp-config` keeps it from booting MCP servers; a child needs no network beyond the model API.
  - Do NOT pass `--add-dir` to a child. Its working directory is the dispatch directory, which is exactly the scope the item should touch, and widening it is how a waiq child ends up committing into cerebruh. A workspace-owned item dispatched to `${BORG_ROOT}` already sees the whole tree; a repo item must not.
  - Do NOT pass `--session-id`. The parent's id is pinned by the runner and reusing it would collide; let each child take a fresh one.
  - Child scope is enforced by the child prompt, the dispatch directory, the recorded-SHA range, and reconciliation — there is no OS-level sandbox behind it (see the header). Treat those four as the actual boundary, not as bookkeeping.
  - Items that edit a slash command under any `.claude/commands/` should have the child run `.bin/sync-codex-prompts.sh` afterward, so the bridged Codex skill stays in sync — Codex is still the interactive alternate harness even though it no longer drives scheduled jobs.
  - **The branch rule — state it in every child prompt.** Claude Code's built-in git guidance tells a session to branch before committing when it is on the default branch, so a child left to its own defaults commits the item's work onto a new side branch and leaves the repository checked out there. Verified in a scratch repo on 2026-08-15: the child committed to `bump-version-0.2.0`, `main` never moved, and the parent's `<recorded>..HEAD` check STILL passed because HEAD had followed the child onto the new branch. That is a silent success report over work that never landed, and the next child in the loop then starts on the previous child's branch. Codex had no such default, so this hazard arrived with the 2026-08-15 migration and has never survived a real run unnoticed.
- The child prompt must be self-contained: the item's full text, its source BACKLOG.md path, and the standing rules — implement in the owning repo following its AGENTS.md, rules, and ADR conventions; verify as far as headless allows (run the repo's tests/build if it has them; never claim verification the item reserves for a device or the user); commit with a clear message ON THE BRANCH THAT IS ALREADY CHECKED OUT — never create, switch, rename, or delete a branch, and never commit to a detached HEAD, regardless of any default guidance about branching before committing; never push to any remote; never write `cerebruh/` wiki content (read-only for all agents per the workspace AGENTS.md — `bypassPermissions` skips the deny rules that would otherwise enforce this, so state the rule in every child prompt); do NOT edit any BACKLOG.md or the plan file (the parent owns trackers); if the item proves infeasible headless, commit nothing and print `INFEASIBLE: <reason>`; otherwise print every commit SHA it created as its final line in the form `CREATED: <sha> [<sha> ...]`.
- Verify the branch FIRST, before reading the SHA range. Run `git -C "<dispatch repo>" rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD` and compare it with the branch recorded at spawn. If they differ, the child broke the branch rule: the `<recorded SHA>..HEAD` range is measured against the wrong ref and will look correct while the item's work sits off the recorded branch. Do NOT check the item off. Report it as `skipped: child committed to branch <name> instead of <recorded>` with both branch names and the commit SHAs, leave the repository exactly as the child left it (switching back is a checkout, which the stray-change rule below forbids), and move to the next item. A branch mismatch is a failed item, not a bookkeeping detail.
- Then verify by evidence and reconciliation. Inspect only commits in `<recorded SHA>..HEAD` in the dispatch repo, then reconcile that range with the SHA(s) in the child's final output and inspect the reconciled commits' contents. The range bounds the candidates but does not prove authorship: a concurrent session can commit inside the window, as happened repeatedly on 2026-08-02. Reconciliation is what exposes that ambiguity. If the range and child output disagree, trust the range as the repository record, report the discrepancy, and do not guess which commits belong to the child.
- Run `git status` in the dispatch repo and report every uncommitted change. Leave all of them exactly as found. Never stage, commit, revert, stash, or check out an uncommitted change during verification, even when it appears to complete the item; on 2026-08-02 concurrent sessions wrote and committed in the same repositories during BURN, so ownership cannot be inferred from timing or relevance.
- Update trackers in the parent: check the item off (or move it to a Done section, per that file's convention) in its source BACKLOG.md and in the plan file; commit the tracker updates. If the child reported infeasible or left no commit, mark the item `skipped: <reason>` in the plan file instead and move on.
Continue until no actionable items remain — or the run dies with the weekly budget, which is the expected exit; the commits and checked boxes are the durable record.

WRAP — only reached if the plan empties before the weekly budget does.
Pipe a summary (items completed / skipped / needs-user, with commit hashes) into `${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/notify-email.sh c4po "Borg backlog burndown — {YYYY-MM-DD} — {C} done, {S} skipped"`. Body header: same as the subject.
